{"name":"Purple Veil","hex":"#d3d5e0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"purple","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour between red and blue; violet, though often closer to magenta.","Any non-spectral colour on the line of purples on a colour chromaticity diagram or a colour wheel between violet and red."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a purple hue.","Not predominantly red or blue, but having a mixture of Democrat and Republican support."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/purple"},{"term":"veil","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Something hung up or spread out to hide or protect the face, or hide an object from view; usually of gauze, crepe, or similar diaphanous material.","Anything that partially obscures a clear view."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To dress in, or decorate with, a veil.","To conceal as with a veil."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/veil"}],"entries":[{"title":"Veil","blocks":[{"text":"A veil is an article of clothing or hanging cloth that is intended to cover some part of the head or face, or an object of some significance. Veiling has a long history in European, Asian, and African societies. The practice has been prominent in different forms in Judaism, Christianity, and Islam. The practice of veiling is especially associated with women and sacred objects, though in some cultures, it is men, rather than women, who are expected to wear a veil. Besides its enduring religious significance, veiling continues to play a role in some modern secular contexts, such as wedding customs."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7b/Chief_Vestal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veil"},{"title":"Purple","blocks":[{"text":"Purple is a color similar in appearance to violet light. In the RYB color model historically used in the arts, purple is a secondary color created by combining red and blue pigments. In the CMYK color model used in modern printing, purple is made by combining magenta pigment with cyan pigment, black pigment, or both. In the RGB color model used in computer and television screens, purple is created by mixing red and blue light in order to create colors that appear similar to violet light. According to color theory, purple is considered a cool color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/330px-Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purple"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Bronze_Statuette_of_a_Veiled_and_Masked_Dancer_1.jpg/500px-Bronze_Statuette_of_a_Veiled_and_Masked_Dancer_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Bronze_Statuette_of_a_Veiled_and_Masked_Dancer_1.jpg/500px-Bronze_Statuette_of_a_Veiled_and_Masked_Dancer_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Greek bronze statuette of a veiled and masked dancer, 2nd–3rd century BC.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABronze_Statuette_of_a_Veiled_and_Masked_Dancer_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Veils_bavaria_ncd_2012.jpg/500px-Veils_bavaria_ncd_2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Veils_bavaria_ncd_2012.jpg/500px-Veils_bavaria_ncd_2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Depiction of Isabeau of Bavaria, queen of France, wearing veiling","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVeils_bavaria_ncd_2012.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/A_Coptic_woman_of_the_Poorer_Class._%281918%29_-_TIMEA.jpg/500px-A_Coptic_woman_of_the_Poorer_Class._%281918%29_-_TIMEA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/A_Coptic_woman_of_the_Poorer_Class._%281918%29_-_TIMEA.jpg/500px-A_Coptic_woman_of_the_Poorer_Class._%281918%29_-_TIMEA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Coptic woman wearing a veil (1918)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_Coptic_woman_of_the_Poorer_Class._(1918)_-_TIMEA.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Targui.jpg/500px-Targui.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Targui.jpg/1280px-Targui.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Targui.jpg/500px-Targui.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tuareg man wearing a veil","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATargui.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Sehrabandi.png/330px-Sehrabandi.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Sehrabandi.png/330px-Sehrabandi.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Indian groom in traditional attire, with Sherwani, Sehra and Mojdi","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASehrabandi.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Women_praying_in_the_Western_Wall_tunnels_by_David_Shankbone.jpg/500px-Women_praying_in_the_Western_Wall_tunnels_by_David_Shankbone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.w